write('reference/timeline.html',enhanced_page('Anchor Timeline',f'''<p class="lead">{tl_rows} entries to real artworks and institutions, with China and other traditions side by side; this is not a “greatest works” list but a set of starting points that connect the method lessons to real materials.</p><section><h2>How to use the timeline</h2><ol><li>Pick one or two works per category; look, listen or read first, then record three pieces of evidence with the observation framework of the method lessons.</li><li>Note simultaneity: different regions in the same century may be solving entirely different problems — do not rank them as “more advanced.”</li><li>Most notes give an institution and a search term (e.g. “search: Maya”): type it into the institution\'s search box to locate the object; a few entries lack a stable institutional page, and the note states what form the source takes (e.g. “public research materials”).</li><li>Collection links point to entry pages of official sites or public-domain archives; the collection page is authoritative for object information — if it disagrees with this page, trust the collection and record the difference.</li><li>This page is not a complete art-history narrative; for the systematic study of history, pair it with a general survey of world and Chinese art.</li></ol><p><a href="glossary.html">Companion: core glossary</a> · <a href="roadmap.html">Five-stage roadmap</a> · <a href="checklist.html">Core concepts self-check list</a></p></section>{''.join(tl_sections)}''',1))

tax_sections=[]
for num,name,*_ in base.DATA:
    groups=''.join(f'<h3>{esc(g)}</h3><p>{esc(t)}</p>' for g,t in TAXONOMY[num].items())
    tax_sections.append(f'<section><h2>{num} · <a href="../{base.FOLDERS[num]}/index.html">{name}</a></h2>{groups}</section>')
TAX_INTRO='''<section><h2>First, an answer: did the previous version list everything?</h2><p>No. The previous version covered the main creative fields but omitted many subfields and mixed some distinct dimensions together. This edition expands the index and states its boundaries, without claiming to exhaust every local tradition, new-media practice or their names worldwide.</p><p>Classification is a tool for asking questions and retrieving. Medium, function, region, history and object of study each yield different, overlapping catalogs; no single tree is both mutually exclusive and able to exhaust all art.</p></section><section><h2>Do not mix the six dimensions</h2><table><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Examples and role</th></tr><tr><td>Medium / form</td><td>Painting, sound, the body, space, computation; answers what organizes experience.</td></tr><tr><td>Subject / genre / use</td><td>Portrait, epic, comedy, ritual, advertising; these concepts sit at different levels and need field-specific interpretation.</td></tr><tr><td>Region / community tradition</td><td>Different regions of China, Europe, South Asia, Southeast Asia, West Asia and North Africa, sub-Saharan Africa, the Americas, Oceania and diaspora communities; each holds vast internal differences.</td></tr><tr><td>History / style</td><td>The names of periods and movements belong within their local histories; European periodization must not be laid over the whole globe.</td></tr><tr><td>Production / dissemination</td><td>Court, religion, community, market, public institutions, online platforms; one work may pass through several systems.</td></tr><tr><td>Theory / professional division of labor</td><td>Aesthetics, history and theory, criticism, education, curating, conservation, management; research fields and creative media are not the same dimension.</td></tr></table></section><section><h2>Relation to official catalogs</h2><p>As an institutional reference, China\'s Ministry of Education 2022 graduate catalog divides professional art degrees into six groups — music, dance, theatre and film, xiqu and quyi, fine arts and calligraphy, and design — and establishes art studies as a discipline. That catalog serves degree administration and is not a classification of all human art. This course takes a broad humanistic view and therefore also includes literature, architecture and landscape as related creative fields.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A22/moe_833/202209/t20220914_660828.html">the 2022 catalog notice</a>, <a href="https://www.moe.gov.cn/fbh/live/2023/55658/dxjy/202312/t20231219_1095135.html">the Art Discipline Committee\'s reading</a>. This page describes that version only and does not claim it equals the latest complete set of all subsequent adjustments.</p><p>The UNESCO intangible-heritage framework attends to mutually crossing domains — oral expression, performance, social practice — which include many art practices and also knowledge and activities whose primary character is not artistic; the ICH lists therefore cannot replace an art taxonomy. See <a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/intangible-heritage-domains-00052">the official domains page</a>.</p></section>'''
write('reference/taxonomy.html',enhanced_page('Expanded Index of Art Categories',TAX_INTRO+''.join(tax_sections)+'''<section><h2>Which border fields exist?</h2><p>Cooking and food, incense and smell, tea and flowers, body adornment, gardening, ritual, videogames and biological media can become art practices in specific contexts, but not all related activities automatically belong to art. When judging, record the concrete practice, the community\'s self-description, the context of presentation and the questions posed; do not count whole trades of life wholesale as new major categories.</p><p>The same object can carry several labels: shadow puppetry can belong at once to form-making, performance, sound, narrative and folk practice. Its main entry in this course is 13, linked to 10, 08, 07 and 03.</p><p><a href="coverage.html">See the coverage status of every entry →</a></p></section>''',1))

level_table=''.join(f'<tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>' for a,b,c in LEVELS)
write('reference/roadmap.html',enhanced_page('From Beginner to Independent Practice and Research',f'''<p class="lead">The five-stage route now comes with full text, assignments and feedback criteria. “Masterwork study” means studying the methods of mature works and sustained independent practice — not a title conferred by reading.</p><section><h2>Nine lessons per category, five stages</h2><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Lessons</th><th>Observable learning outcomes</th></tr>{level_table}</table><p>Prerequisites run in sequence. Experienced learners may submit outcomes for the relevant stage to self-check and skip ahead after filling the gaps; for wholly unfamiliar fields, starting from lesson 01 is still recommended.</p></section><section><h2>Two study tracks</h2><h3>Humanities track</h3><p>First complete lessons 01–03 of all 14 categories to build the panorama, then take three categories through lesson 07; at the advanced stage choose artwork analysis, comparative study or a curatorial project rather than becoming a professional maker in each one.</p><h3>Specialization track</h3><p>Choose one subfield and concentrate lessons 04–09 on the same theme, completing project after project. After each round, add that field\'s history, professional technique and original works; build regular feedback with experienced practitioners. This course does not replace the professional studio, music or physical training.</p></section><section><h2>Why a syllabus is not completed master training</h2><p>Advanced ability requires transfer across subjects, long repeated revision, peer and mentor criticism, and real viewing and use. Finishing the text, answering quiz questions and imitating prompts cannot, alone, prove independent judgment.</p><p>Each project round should deliver four things: a reviewable outcome, a traceable process, the strongest objection with your response, and the question for the next round. Only after at least two rounds hold on different objects should you widen the scope.</p></section><section><h2>The yearly studio: repeat without repeating yourself</h2><ol><li>Round one: limit the medium and the scale, finish one small project, find your weakest ability.</li><li>Round two: add basic exercises around that weakness, then a new project on a different object.</li><li>Round three: contrast with a different tradition or medium to test the boundaries of the original method.</li><li>Round four: assemble a portfolio or research archive, invite criticism from people who know the field, and write the questions for next year.</li></ol><p>Rounds need not map onto calendar months — proceed by actual time and feedback; fluency without evidence does not automatically promote to mastery.</p></section><section><h2>Project self-assessment and external feedback</h2>{RUBRIC}<p><a href="method.html">Basic method card</a> · <a href="checklist.html">Core concepts self-check list</a> · <a href="glossary.html">Core glossary</a> · <a href="timeline.html">Anchor timeline</a> · <a href="coverage.html">Course coverage boundaries</a></p><p>Method lessons need real materials: the glossary and the timeline provide the content layer; for a full art-history narrative, pair this course with a general survey.</p></section>''',1))

write('index.html',enhanced_page('Start from the Work, toward Independent Judgment',f'''<p class="lead">14 categories and the foundations unit — 126 lessons. From introductory observation to themed creation, research and masterwork study. Every level centers on exercise, evidence and revision.</p><section><h2>Choose your entry point</h2><nav><a href="{base.FOLDERS['00']}/lessons/01.html">Start from zero</a><a href="reference/roadmap.html">Five-stage study route</a><a href="reference/checklist.html">Core concepts self-check list</a><a href="reference/glossary.html">Core glossary</a><a href="reference/timeline.html">Anchor timeline</a><a href="reference/taxonomy.html">Expanded category index</a><a href="reference/coverage.html">Course coverage chart</a></nav><p>The taxonomy index registers {count} entries covering media, forms, materials, professional divisions and research fields, with overlaps allowed. What has been written are the shared category courses and {len(MICRO)} group micro-lessons; dedicated full specialty courses for every subfield do not all exist yet.</p></section><h2>Every category has the full stage route</h2><div class="grid">{''.join(outline)}</div><section><h2>How to start without drowning in the numbers</h2><p>Read lessons 01–03 of the fundamentals unit first, then take any category through lessons 01–05. Each time, keep a concrete artwork observation, a controlled exercise and revision notes; then decide whether to go deeper into lessons 06–09. You need not take every category at once.</p><p>The course reads offline and the interactive demos call no external services; institutional works, recordings and external reading need a connection. Open assignments must actually be done by you — the course automatically grades no artistic quality.</p></section>'''))

